window.__hvBdRegister("cyropaedia", {"key":"cyropaedia","sections":[{"i":1,"j":23,"loc":"1.1.1–1.2.16","title":"Why Cyrus ruled men, and Persian education","summary":"Xenophon opens by noting how often governments of every kind are overthrown and how herds obey their keepers more readily than men obey rulers. The case of Cyrus the Persian, whom vast and distant nations obeyed willingly, makes him conclude that ruling men is possible if done with intelligence, and he sets out to examine Cyrus's birth, nature and education. He then describes the Persian system: a public square divided among boys, youths, mature men and elders, each under chosen officers. Boys learn justice, self-control and obedience; youths guard the city and hunt as training for war; mature men serve in arms and supply the magistrates; elders judge cases. Only those who complete each stage may rise to the next.","ideas":["Men conspire against rulers more readily than herds against herdsmen, yet Cyrus won willing obedience","Persian laws aim to form character from the start rather than merely punish crimes","Four age classes, with promotion only for those who complete each stage","Hunting at public expense as the best preparation for war"],"terms":[{"t":"Free Square","d":"The Persian public square, cleared of traders, where the age classes gathered daily under their officers."},{"t":"ingratitude","d":"An offence for which Persian boys were tried and punished, as the companion of shamelessness."},{"t":"the elders","d":"Men over about fifty who had passed every stage without blame; they judged cases and elected officers."}]},{"i":24,"j":41,"loc":"1.3.1–1.3.18","title":"The boy Cyrus at Astyages' Median court","summary":"At about twelve Cyrus travels with his mother Mandane to her father Astyages, king of the Medes, and is delighted by Median finery and horses. At dinner he contrasts the simple Persian road to satiety with the Median maze of dishes, gives his meat away to servants, and mocks the cupbearer Sacas and the drunken birthday banquet. Offered horses, hunting and free access to his grandfather, he chooses to stay to learn riding. When Mandane asks how he will learn justice, he recounts being flogged for judging the case of the two tunics by fit rather than by lawful ownership. Mandane warns that Median rule is closer to tyranny than to Persian equality under law.","ideas":["Persian frugality contrasted with Median luxury and adornment","Cyrus's generosity and wit in dividing food and imitating the cupbearer","The tunic case: justice judged by lawful title, not by convenience","Persian kingship under law versus Median rule by the king's will"],"terms":[{"t":"isēgoria (equal freedom of speech)","d":"Cyrus jokingly applies the term to the drunken guests who all talked and none listened."},{"t":"tyranny","d":"Mandane's name for the principle that one man should have more than all, which she sets against Persian equality."}]},{"i":42,"j":56,"loc":"1.4.1–1.4.15","title":"Cyrus grows up hunting in Media","summary":"Cyrus wins over the Median boys, their fathers and his grandfather through kindness and attentiveness. Xenophon describes his early talkativeness, which turns to modesty and blushing as he reaches youth; he challenges friends in the contests where he is weakest and laughs at his defeats until he excels at riding. Having emptied the royal park of game, he is allowed to hunt in the wild with his uncle Cyaxares, where he recklessly chases a deer and kills a boar. He later uses a clever question about runaway slaves to ask leave to hunt with his friends, and Astyages finally organizes a great hunt at which Cyrus insists all his companions compete freely.","ideas":["Cyrus's talkativeness shows naïveté and affection, then gives way to modesty","He competes where he is weakest and improves through persistence","Wild hunting as a test of daring, reckless at first","He prefers his comrades' shared rivalry to a privileged hunt"],"terms":[]},{"i":57,"j":69,"loc":"1.4.16–1.4.28","title":"First battle against the Assyrian raiders; farewell to Media","summary":"At fifteen or sixteen Cyrus sees his first fighting when the Assyrian crown prince, hunting before his wedding, raids Median territory. Cyrus, newly armed, urges a charge on the scattered foragers; Cyaxares leads it with Cyrus out in front, and Astyages follows up, putting the Assyrian cavalry to flight. Astyages is pleased with the victory but troubled by Cyrus's frenzied daring and his gloating over the dead. Cambyses then recalls Cyrus to finish his Persian training; he leaves amid tears, giving away Astyages' gifts and insisting the recipients keep them. The book ends with the story of the Median kinsman who invents excuses to keep kissing him goodbye.","ideas":["Cyrus spots the enemy's weakness and proposes the decisive charge","His reckless pursuit compared to an untrained hound","Generosity at parting: gifts must stay with his friends","The lovesick Mede and the Persian kiss of kinship"],"terms":[]},{"i":70,"j":83,"loc":"1.5.1–1.5.14","title":"Cyrus chosen to lead the Persian army","summary":"Back in Persia, Cyrus quickly wins respect from boys who expected Median softness and excels among the youths. After Astyages dies, Cyaxares succeeds him and the Assyrian king assembles a coalition against Medes and Persians, including Croesus of Lydia. Cyaxares asks for Persian help, and the elders appoint Cyrus commander, with two hundred peers who each pick four more, and each of the thousand thirty commoners. Cyrus's first speech to the peers argues that virtue is practised for the sake of future reward and that to train and never use it is folly. He adds that their cause is just, that they fight in self-defence and for friends, and that he has the gods' favour.","ideas":["The Assyrian king builds an alliance by persuasion and bribes","The peers and the commoners: how Cyrus's army was raised","Virtue is toiled for in order to reap its fruits, like a harvest","A just war of defence and help to friends"],"terms":[{"t":"homotimoi (peers)","d":"The Persians fully educated in the public system, from whom officers and magistrates came."}]},{"i":84,"j":98,"loc":"1.6.1–1.6.15","title":"Cambyses advises Cyrus: gods, supplies, generalship","summary":"Cyrus sets out after favourable omens, and his father Cambyses walks with him in a long conversation. Cambyses reminds him that he was taught divination so as not to depend on seers, and both agree one should honour the gods in prosperity and ask only for what one has prepared oneself to deserve. Cambyses warns him not to rely on Cyaxares for supplies but to secure provisions himself and to plan against want while in plenty. Cyrus recalls how his father once showed that the teacher who taught him generalship had taught only tactics, ignoring supplies, health, morale and obedience.","ideas":["Praying only for what one has made oneself fit to receive","A general must secure provisions before need arises","Tactics is only a small part of generalship","Governing others well is the greatest and most admirable task"],"terms":[{"t":"mantikē (the soothsayer's art)","d":"Divination, which Cambyses had Cyrus learn so he could read the gods' signs himself."}]},{"i":99,"j":110,"loc":"1.6.16–1.6.27","title":"Health, morale, obedience and the love of subjects","summary":"Cambyses tells Cyrus that a general must prevent sickness, not just bring physicians, by choosing healthy camps and keeping soldiers exercised and never idle. They discuss prizes and contests for training, and Cambyses warns against raising false hopes, which lose credit like a hunter's call misused. Obedience can be compelled by reward and punishment, but willing obedience comes from being thought wiser about men's interests, and the shortest road to seeming wise is to be wise. Affection is won by sharing in subjects' fortunes and by out-enduring them. When Cyrus asks how to gain an advantage over the enemy, Cambyses startles him by saying the general must be a cheat and a thief.","ideas":["An idle army is the heaviest burden","Hopes must not be raised falsely, or words lose their force","Willing obedience goes to the one believed wiser","To seem wise, really be wise in the matter at hand"],"terms":[{"t":"willing obedience","d":"Obedience given freely to a leader thought to understand the followers' interests better than they do."}]},{"i":111,"j":129,"loc":"1.6.28–1.6.46","title":"Deceiving enemies, and the limits of human wisdom","summary":"Cambyses explains that Persian boys practised trickery on beasts in hunting, not on men, so that they would harm no friends yet be ready for war. He tells of an ancient teacher who taught boys to deceive both friends and enemies, with the result that they cheated friends, so the law now teaches only truthfulness until the age when deceit toward enemies can be taught safely. He advises catching the enemy disordered, unarmed or asleep, feigning flight, and inventing new stratagems, drawing on Cyrus's own methods of snaring birds and hares. He ends that human wisdom chooses no better than casting lots, while the gods know all things and counsel whom they favour.","ideas":["Deceit and taking advantage are just toward enemies, not friends","Why boys learn only truthfulness first","Stratagems learned from bird-snaring and hare-hunting","Men act by conjecture; only the gods foresee outcomes"],"terms":[{"t":"pleonexia (taking advantage)","d":"Getting the better of others by craft, which Cambyses permits against enemies and forbids among friends."}]},{"i":130,"j":149,"loc":"2.1.1–2.1.19","title":"Cyrus arms the Persian commoners for close combat","summary":"Cyrus reaches Cyaxares, who lists the enemy coalition: Croesus, the Phrygians, Cappadocians, Arabians and Assyrians, far outnumbering the Medes and Armenians. Since more Persians would not close the gap, Cyrus proposes giving the Persian commoners the peers' close-combat arms of corselet, shield and sabre, so that they can fight hand to hand. He tells the peers they must steel the commoners' hearts, and then offers the commoners equal arms, equal dangers and equal shares in success. All the commoners accept and enrol with the peers.","ideas":["Fighting at long range favours the larger army","Close-combat arms make courage, not numbers or skill, decisive","Commoners invited to share the peers' arms, danger and rewards"],"terms":[{"t":"peltasts","d":"Light-armed troops with small shields, numerous in both armies."}]},{"i":150,"j":178,"loc":"2.1.20–2.2.17","title":"Training the army and jokes at Cyrus's table","summary":"Cyrus has supplies provided so the soldiers can drill only with close-combat arms, and he sets up competitions with promotions for privates, corporals, sergeants, lieutenants and captains and prizes for whole units. Companies tent together to build acquaintance and order, eat only after working up a sweat, and are invited in turn to Cyrus's dinners, where all eat what he eats. At one dinner Hystaspas tells of a greedy recruit who lost his meat, and another captain of recruits who took orders too literally. When the austere Aglaitadas calls such tales humbug, Cyrus distinguishes boastful lying from harmless wit, and Aglaitadas argues that those who make us weep do more good than those who make us laugh.","ideas":["Competition and promotion as incentives at every rank","Tenting and messing together build comradeship and order","Humbug is lying for gain; amusing stories are wit","Aglaitadas: tears teach, laughter does not"],"terms":[{"t":"alazōn (humbug)","d":"A pretender who claims more wealth, courage or ability than he has, for gain."}]},{"i":179,"j":216,"loc":"2.2.18–2.3.24","title":"Rewards by merit, and companies at drill","summary":"Chrysantas objects that good and bad should not share alike, and Cyrus decides to put the question of reward by merit to the army. He argues that the base must be weeded out, since vice attracts with present pleasures while virtue leads uphill, and that recruits should be chosen from any source. In the assembly Chrysantas says even a weak man gains when the strong are rewarded, and the commoner Pheraulas argues that close combat rests on natural instinct and that commoners, trained by necessity, can compete with the peers. The army votes that each be rewarded according to desert, with Cyrus as judge. Cyrus then invites to dinner companies that drill well, including a sham battle with clods and cudgels.","ideas":["Equal shares for good and bad is unjust and weakens valour","Vice leads downhill and attracts many; virtue leads uphill","Pheraulas: fighting is natural, and necessity trained the commoners","Drill folded into daily routine, such as marching in to mess"],"terms":[{"t":"reward according to desert","d":"The rule, adopted by vote, that each man's share depends on his services, with Cyrus as judge."}]},{"i":217,"j":248,"loc":"2.4.1–2.4.32","title":"The Indian embassy and the plan against Armenia","summary":"Summoned to meet Indian envoys, Cyrus arrives promptly with his army in plain Persian dress and tells Cyaxares that speed and obedience honour him more than finery. The Indian king offers to side with whichever party was wronged, and Cyrus proposes him as arbitrator. Cyrus argues that friends are won by kindness and gifts and asks for funds, suggesting they force the Armenian king, who has stopped paying tribute and sending troops, back into line. Under cover of a great hunt and Cyaxares's visit to the outposts, Cyrus leads his men to the border after an eagle omen. He sends Chrysantas to seize the mountains where the Armenian takes refuge, and sends a messenger openly demanding the tribute.","ideas":["Sweat and speed as better adornment than purple robes","Willing followers must be won by kind words and services","A hunt as cover for a military stroke against Armenia","The campaign planned like a hunt: beaters and nets"],"terms":[]},{"i":249,"j":276,"loc":"3.1.1–3.1.27","title":"Trial of the Armenian king; Tigranes pleads","summary":"The Armenian king, conscious of his wrongs, sends his family and treasure to the mountains, where Chrysantas captures them; he himself is trapped on a hill and comes down to stand trial. His elder son Tigranes, Cyrus's old hunting companion, arrives. Under Cyrus's questioning the king truthfully admits he broke his treaty for the sake of liberty, and by his own standards deserves death. Tigranes argues that to kill friends when they have become discreet harms Cyrus, that defeat and fear teach discretion better than blows, and that Cyrus may take whatever security he wishes.","ideas":["Cyrus makes the king condemn himself by his own rules of justice","Tigranes: discretion (sōphrosynē) makes every other quality useful","Fear crushes the spirit more than actual punishment","Debate over whether discretion can arise in a single day"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosynē (discretion)","d":"Self-control or good sense, which Tigranes claims his father has acquired through defeat."}]},{"i":277,"j":292,"loc":"3.1.28–3.1.43","title":"Pardon and alliance with Armenia","summary":"Tigranes argues that no one would be more grateful than his pardoned father, and that the existing government can best supply troops and money. Cyrus asks for half the Armenian army and double the arrears of tribute plus a loan, then frees the king's wife and children and Tigranes's wife without ransom. After dinner Tigranes tells how his father executed the philosopher who taught him; the man forgave the king, saying wrongs done in ignorance are involuntary. The king admits he was jealous, Cyrus calls the fault human, and father and son are reconciled. Tigranes's wife says she looked only at the man who offered his life for her, and Tigranes joins Cyrus's army.","ideas":["Gratitude from the pardoned makes the surest friends","Captives returned free, without ransom","The executed philosopher: wrongdoing from ignorance is involuntary","Tigranes and his wife, devoted to each other"],"terms":[]},{"i":293,"j":323,"loc":"3.2.1–3.2.31","title":"Seizing the Chaldaean heights and making peace","summary":"Cyrus surveys the mountains from which the warlike, poor Chaldaeans raid Armenia, leaving its land uncultivated. He storms the heights: the Armenians feign flight, the Chaldaeans pursue, and the Persian swordsmen rout them. Cyrus builds a fort, frees and treats the prisoners, and offers the Chaldaeans peace or war. He then arranges a treaty in which Chaldaeans farm idle Armenian land for rent, Armenians pasture on Chaldaean mountains, both intermarry, and Cyrus holds the fort to side with whichever party is wronged. He hires Chaldaean mercenaries and sends an envoy with Armenian and Chaldaean companions to ask the Indian king for money.","ideas":["Speed seizes the heights before the enemy can gather","Mercy to prisoners opens the way to peace","Mutual tillage and pasturage turn enemies into partners","A neutral garrison guarantees the treaty"],"terms":[]},{"i":324,"j":351,"loc":"3.3.1–3.3.28","title":"Return to Media and invasion of Assyria","summary":"Cyrus refuses the Armenian queen's gifts, telling her not to bury treasure, and leaves Armenia hailed as a benefactor. In Media he distributes money through his captains and tells his men that their prosperity came from toil, watchfulness and speed. Seeing his army ready and its rivalries turning to jealousy, he persuades Cyaxares to invade the enemy's country at once, arguing that attacking spares Median land, feeds the army and raises morale, since battles are decided more by souls than bodies. After sacrifices to the gods and heroes of Media and Assyria they cross, plunder and advance, using fires to deceive the enemy's scouts. The Assyrians entrench in the open while Cyrus camps hidden behind hills.","ideas":["Good deeds not done for pay; treasure is not for burying","Shared danger cures jealousy among rivals","Taking the offensive raises courage and spreads fear","Concealment makes an army more terrifying when it appears"],"terms":[{"t":"parasang","d":"A Persian measure of distance, roughly three and a half miles."}]},{"i":352,"j":393,"loc":"3.3.29–3.3.70","title":"First battle with the Assyrians at their entrenchments","summary":"Cyrus dissuades Cyaxares from parading before the Assyrian camp, preferring to keep the enemy uneasy and to strike when they come out. After favourable sacrifice he has the peers exhort the new comrades and charges the rear-rank officers to urge on the line, while the Assyrian king tells his men that victory saves their lives and goods. Cyrus tells Chrysantas that a speech on the spot cannot make untrained men brave; only laws, teachers and habit can. Pressed by Cyaxares, he attacks with the watchword Zeus our Helper and Guide and a paean, and the Persians charge at a run. The Assyrians flee into their camp; when the kings rally at the gates, Cyrus orders a disciplined withdrawal out of range.","ideas":["Waiting until enough of the enemy come out to make the victory decisive","Exhortation cannot replace training, laws and example","The charge driven by rivalry, discipline and shared cheering","The peers' orderly retreat, each knowing his place like a chorus"],"terms":[{"t":"paean","d":"The hymn sung before battle, which Cyrus starts and his whole army joins."}]},{"i":394,"j":418,"loc":"4.1.1–4.1.24","title":"After the victory: Cyrus asks to pursue","summary":"Cyrus thanks the gods, praises his Persians and promotes Chrysantas, who obeyed the order to retreat even with his sword raised. Overnight the demoralized Assyrians and their allies, Croesus among them, abandon their camp. Cyrus and his captains urge Cyaxares to pursue, but Cyaxares, feasting and perhaps jealous, argues for moderation in success and warns that fleeing enemies defending their families can be made desperate. Cyrus asks only for volunteers, and the Mede who once claimed kinship with him undertakes to rally followers.","ideas":["Obedience, as shown by Chrysantas, saves both the soldier and his men","Cyaxares counsels moderation in the pleasure of success and fears a cornered enemy","Cyrus asks for willing volunteers rather than compelling anyone"],"terms":[{"t":"Cyaxares","d":"King of the Medes and Cyrus's uncle, here shown as cautious, self-indulgent and touchy about his authority."}]},{"i":419,"j":465,"loc":"4.2.1–4.2.47","title":"Hyrcanian defection and the night attack","summary":"The Hyrcanians, ill-used rearguard of the Assyrians, offer to guide Cyrus against the retreating enemy; he pledges them equal standing with Persians and Medes. Almost all the Medes volunteer out of affection or hope of gain, and a light from heaven is said to shine on the night march. At dawn the surprised enemy camp collapses without resistance, and Cyrus disarms those inside. He orders the captured commissaries to prepare food for the returning pursuers and persuades his Persians, with Hystaspas's support, to wait for their allies and leave the division of treasure to them.","ideas":["Cyrus trusts in his own strength rather than waiting for hostages","Plundering in the hour of victory turns a soldier into a camp-follower","Self-restraint over food and spoil wins the lasting loyalty of allies","Appetites are bad men's masters but good men's servants"],"terms":[{"t":"Hyrcanians","d":"Cavalry people subject to the Assyrians who defect to Cyrus and act as his guides."},{"t":"commissaries","d":"Camp servants in charge of tents and provisions, whom Cyrus puts to work feeding his army."}]},{"i":466,"j":501,"loc":"4.3.1–4.4.13","title":"Persians become horsemen; captives set free","summary":"Watching the Median and Hyrcanian horsemen bring in wagons and noblewomen, Cyrus argues that the Persians can claim no spoil by their own strength without cavalry, and that captured horses and gear make one possible. Chrysantas welcomes the plan, likening the horseman to a Centaur that can be taken apart and put together again. The Persians vote never to be seen on foot if given a horse, a custom the narrator says persists. When the riders return with prisoners, Cyrus releases them on promise of disarmament and obedience, assuring them of their homes, farms and families.","ideas":["Without cavalry the Persians depend on their allies for any victory's fruits","Grown men learn better than boys and have the leisure for war","The rider as a detachable Centaur with four eyes and four ears","An inhabited country is valuable, so conquered people are left on their land"],"terms":[{"t":"Centaur","d":"Chrysantas's image for the horseman, combining human forethought with the horse's speed and strength."}]},{"i":502,"j":535,"loc":"4.5.1–4.5.34","title":"Cyaxares' anger and Cyrus's letter","summary":"The Persians keep a frugal dinner and set ambushes that catch deserters, while the Medes revel. Cyaxares, waking to find his camp emptied, angrily sends a messenger to recall the Medes. Cyrus sends a senior officer to Persia for reinforcements, has the Hyrcanian king entertain the messenger, and calms the Medes by saying their king's anger will pass with his fear. His letter tells Cyaxares that driving the enemy far away protects him best, reproaches him for recalling all his men, and advises him not to threaten large numbers.","ideas":["Hunger is the Persians' relish and the river their drink","Cyaxares rages at being left deserted and summons the Medes with threats","No one is deserted by friends who are conquering his enemies","Threatening many at once teaches them to disregard the ruler"],"terms":[]},{"i":536,"j":571,"loc":"4.5.35–4.6.12","title":"Dividing the spoil; Gobryas seeks vengeance","summary":"Cyrus has the Medes and Hyrcanians divide the spoil and tribute money, pay cavalry twice what infantry get, and keep the camp market open; in return he asks for the captured horses to mount his Persians, joking about their falls. He frees enslaved captives of many nations to serve as squires. The aged Assyrian Gobryas then arrives as a suppliant: the new Assyrian king killed his only son out of jealousy on a hunt, and he offers his castle, tribute, cavalry and daughter in return for vengeance. Cyrus gives his pledge, and the Medes choose for him the lady of Susa and two music-girls.","ideas":["Mutual trust lets each side act for the other without oversight","Persians accept horses rather than luxuries, being reared rustically","Gobryas's son is murdered for outshooting the prince on a hunt","Cyrus avenges a suppliant on condition that he proves truthful"],"terms":[{"t":"magi","d":"Persian priests who direct what share of the spoil is set apart for the gods."}]},{"i":572,"j":602,"loc":"5.1.1–5.1.30","title":"The lady of Susa and the debate on love","summary":"Cyrus entrusts the captive wife of Abradatas of Susa to his boyhood friend Araspas, who describes her beauty and grief. Cyrus refuses even to look at her for fear of neglecting his duties. Araspas argues that love is a matter of free will, unlike hunger or fire, and that only weaklings blame beauty; Cyrus answers that love enslaves people against their wishes and that beauty kindles fire even from afar. Araspas, tending her, falls desperately in love. Cyrus then tells the Medes they may go home but he will keep faith with the Hyrcanians and Gobryas, and they all choose to stay, one comparing him to a queen bee.","ideas":["Araspas: love is voluntary and the good can restrain desire","Cyrus: love binds like iron fetters, so the wise avoid gazing on beauty","Araspas's confidence is undone as he falls in love","The Medes follow Cyrus willingly, as bees follow their leader"],"terms":[{"t":"Araspas","d":"A Mede, Cyrus's friend from boyhood, given charge of the captive lady."}]},{"i":603,"j":639,"loc":"5.2.1–5.2.37","title":"At Gobryas's castle; resolve to march on Babylon","summary":"Cyrus rides with his new Persian cavalry to Gobryas's impregnable castle, where Gobryas offers treasure and his mourning daughter. Cyrus returns the treasure as her dowry, saying the true gift is a chance to prove himself trustworthy. Dining in camp on straw, Gobryas observes Persian temperance and courtesy and concludes that his people own more but are worth less. Learning that the Cadusians, Sacians and a nobleman the king castrated are the Assyrian's enemies, Cyrus rejects Gobryas's caution and decides to march straight on Babylon while the enemy are still afraid.","ideas":["Being trusted with wealth and power reveals whether a man is just","Persians make themselves capable rather than amass possessions","Large frightened armies panic more as their numbers grow","Battles are decided by good fighting, not numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"Cadusians and Sacians","d":"Powerful peoples hostile to the Assyrian king whom Cyrus hopes to bring into alliance."}]},{"i":640,"j":667,"loc":"5.3.1–5.3.28","title":"Gadatas betrays the frontier fort","summary":"Cyrus gives Gobryas the bulk of the plunder to show that gentlemen need no gold, then marches to Babylon; the Assyrian king answers Gobryas's challenge with insult and refuses battle for a month. Through Gobryas, Cyrus arranges a ruse with the eunuch Gadatas: a feigned attack lets Gadatas enter the frontier fort as a defender and hand it over. The fort is shared among the allies, and the Cadusians, Sacians and Hyrcanians join in strength. When Gadatas hears the king plans to invade his land, he hurries home and Cyrus promises to follow.","ideas":["Outdoing benefactors in benefits as a matter of policy","In war, seeming an enemy can most help a friend","The captured fort becomes a common outwork against Assyria","A large new force of Cadusians and Sacians joins Cyrus"],"terms":[{"t":"Gadatas","d":"An Assyrian prince castrated by the new king, who revolts and becomes Cyrus's ally."}]},{"i":668,"j":698,"loc":"5.3.29–5.3.59","title":"Night march to aid Gadatas","summary":"Cyrus persuades the allies to help Gadatas, arguing that surpassing friends in kindness and enemies in harm will make many want to be friends and none enemies. He sets an exact order of march for a night advance, putting the slow heavy troops in front so the column stays together, and calls for silence. The narrator explains Cyrus's habit of naming every officer, since commands given to no one in particular go unobeyed. The march proceeds through the night with scouts ahead and Cyrus riding the line.","ideas":["Gratitude to a benefactor is both just and advantageous","The slowest troops lead at night so the line is not broken","A general should know his officers by name as a craftsman knows his tools","Orders addressed to 'someone' spread blame and get nothing done"],"terms":[]},{"i":699,"j":749,"loc":"5.4.1–5.4.51","title":"Ambush of Gadatas and the march past Babylon","summary":"One of Gadatas's officers betrays him to the Assyrian king, who ambushes Gadatas; the traitor wounds him, but Cyrus arrives and routs the Assyrians. A Cadusian prince who raids alone is killed, and Cyrus draws the lesson never to detach a force without informing the main body, then avenges the dead. He arranges a truce sparing farmers on both sides. Gadatas, fearing for his life near Babylon, joins Cyrus with his household. Cyrus marches past Babylon at a distance, explaining that a column marching by is weaker than one marching up, and takes three Syrian frontier forts.","ideas":["Cyrus rescues Gadatas though he owes him no personal debt","A detached force must keep the main body informed","Peace for the farmers, war for the men under arms","Marching past a city differs from marching up to it"],"terms":[]},{"i":750,"j":797,"loc":"5.5.1–5.5.48","title":"Cyrus and Cyaxares reconciled","summary":"Cyaxares dismisses the arriving Persian reinforcements and meets Cyrus with a small escort; humiliated, he refuses Cyrus's kiss and weeps. Seated under palm-trees, Cyrus goes through his conduct step by step and asks what wrong he has done, while Cyaxares falls silent. Cyaxares replies that the benefits shame him, comparing Cyrus to a man who wins over another's dogs, guards or wife, and says he values his honour above an enlarged realm. Cyrus asks him to suspend blame, they kiss, and the Medes bring Cyaxares gifts at Cyrus's prompting; Cyrus then urges his friends to persuade the allies to stay.","ideas":["Being angry with all subjects at once makes them all enemies","Cyrus's question-by-question defence of his conduct","For Cyaxares, benefits from a stronger man bring disgrace","A ruler holds his throne by being thought better than his subjects"],"terms":[]},{"i":798,"j":828,"loc":"6.1.1–6.1.30","title":"Council of war and the scythed chariots","summary":"At a council in Cyaxares' presence, the Hyrcanian, the Cadusian, Artabazus and Gobryas all argue for continuing the war. Cyrus explains that winter and lack of supplies are the real threat and proposes taking and building forts, garrisoned by the Persians near the enemy, so the country is held rather than merely crossed. Siege engines are commissioned. On news that the Assyrian has gone to Lydia to form a coalition, Cyrus completes his cavalry and replaces the old Trojan-style chariot with a heavy scythed war-chariot whose driver fights from a turret.","ideas":["Life at home was war, Artabazus jokes, while campaigning is a feast","Forts turn a raiding army into masters of the land, unlike sailors on the sea","The old chariot wasted the best men as skirmishers","The scythed chariot is designed to be hurled into the enemy"],"terms":[{"t":"scythed chariot","d":"Cyrus's war-chariot with strong wheels, a turret-box for the armoured driver and blades on the axles."}]},{"i":829,"j":853,"loc":"6.1.31–6.1.55","title":"Araspas's fall, Panthea, and Abradatas","summary":"Araspas, in love with the lady, threatens force; she reports it to Cyrus, who laughs, forbids violence and privately forgives Araspas, blaming himself. He sends Araspas to Lydia as a sham deserter to spy and to mislead the enemy. Araspas says he has learned from Eros that he has two souls, one good and one bad. The lady, Panthea, sends for her husband Abradatas, who comes over with a thousand horse in gratitude for Cyrus's restraint and builds a hundred chariots. Cyrus devises eight-pole towers drawn by oxen.","ideas":["Cyrus treats love as a power even gods yield to","Araspas's doctrine of two souls, good and bad","Panthea's fidelity brings her husband over as Cyrus's ally","Mobile siege towers prove easier to draw than baggage"],"terms":[{"t":"Panthea","d":"The lady of Susa, wife of Abradatas, whom Cyrus keeps unharmed for her husband."},{"t":"Erōs","d":"Love, called by Araspas a crooked sophist who taught him he has two souls."}]},{"i":854,"j":894,"loc":"6.2.1–6.2.41","title":"News of Croesus's coalition; provisions for the march","summary":"Envoys from the Indian king bring money, and Cyrus sends some of them to spy on the enemy. He trains his forces through hunts and contests until he has ten thousand horse and three hundred scythed chariots. The spies report that Croesus commands a vast coalition, including Egyptians, Thracians and Asian Greeks, mustering for Thymbrara; the army is dismayed. Cyrus rallies them by asking how they would fear their own new arms, and Chrysantas speaks of Lydia's riches. Cyrus then orders twenty days' provisions, a gradual change from wine to water, tools, craftsmen and rules for merchants.","ideas":["Ambassadors learn more than spies disguised as slaves","Croesus's coalition gathers many peoples against Cyrus","Change one's habits gradually, as God moves the seasons","Detailed provision for supplies, tools and trades"],"terms":[{"t":"Croesus","d":"King of Lydia, chosen commander-in-chief of the coalition against Cyrus."}]},{"i":895,"j":931,"loc":"6.3.1–6.3.37","title":"Araspas returns; Cyrus's battle order","summary":"The army marches with Cyaxares left behind with a third of the Medes; scouts and captured foragers locate the enemy two parasangs away, marshalled by Croesus. Araspas returns and Cyrus reveals to all that his desertion was a mission. Araspas reports a front about forty stadia long, thirty deep, with the Egyptians a hundred deep, and Croesus's plan to outflank Cyrus. Cyrus sets a shallow line, with hoplites in front, spearmen and archers behind and a veteran rearguard, then towers, baggage and women's carriages to widen any encircling movement. Abradatas volunteers to face the Egyptians and wins the post by lot.","ideas":["Better to overestimate the enemy and find them fewer","Too deep a phalanx cannot bring its weapons to bear","The rearguard must be more feared by cowards than the enemy","Abradatas takes the post opposite the Egyptians"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The main battle line of infantry, which Cyrus draws up shallow so that every rank is useful."}]},{"i":932,"j":951,"loc":"6.4.1–6.4.20","title":"Panthea arms Abradatas; Cyrus's final address","summary":"The army arms itself in bronze and purple. Panthea gives Abradatas golden armour made from her own jewels, says she would rather die with a brave husband than live disgraced, and recalls Cyrus's honourable treatment of her. Abradatas prays to be worthy of Panthea and of Cyrus, and she follows his chariot until he sends her back. After favourable omens, Cyrus tells his officers that his men are better trained and trust one another, while the enemy are full of traitors; he describes how chariots, cavalry and towers will overwhelm the poorly armed Egyptians, and bids each officer show fearlessness.","ideas":["Panthea calls her husband her most precious jewel","Cyrus kept Panthea as one would a brother's wife","Men who trust each other stand together; the distrustful scheme to flee","The Egyptians' huge shields and depth will hinder them"],"terms":[]},{"i":952,"j":974,"loc":"Book VII 7.1.1–7.1.22","title":"Cyrus reviews his lines before Thymbrara","summary":"After sacrifice and a thunderclap on the right, Cyrus leads out his army under the golden eagle ensign against Croesus. Croesus halts his centre and wheels his wings outward to envelop the Persians on three sides; Cyrus explains to Chrysantas that the plan suits what the enemy sees but not what it cannot see. He rides along the lines with short words of encouragement to each group, assures Abradatas, who has the front place with the chariots, that the flanks will be cleared before he charges, and gives specific orders to Hystaspas, the chariot commander on the flank, and Artagerses and Pharnuchus behind the women's carriages, who are to send the camels against the enemy cavalry.","ideas":["Croesus's gamma-shaped flanking manoeuvre and why its wings stretch so far","Cyrus's varied exhortations: victory as a shared feast and its prizes","Abradatas's chariots held back until the enemy flanks break","The camels positioned to panic the enemy horses"],"terms":[{"t":"paean","d":"The battle hymn Cyrus will start as the signal to close with the enemy."},{"t":"phalanx","d":"A close-ordered battle line of heavy infantry, strongest when it strikes an enemy's flank."}]},{"i":975,"j":1001,"loc":"Book VII 7.1.23–7.1.49","title":"The battle of Thymbrara and the Egyptians","summary":"Croesus's three phalanxes close in, and Cyrus's army stands enclosed like a small tile inside a large one. At the paean Cyrus strikes the enemy flank; the camels rout the horses, the chariots charge, and Abradatas with his close friends drives through into the Egyptian phalanx, where he is thrown and killed. The Egyptians, with their long spears and great shields, push the Persians back to the towers until Cyrus rides round to their rear; unhorsed and remounted, he sees from a tower that only the Egyptians still stand. Admiring their courage, he stops the fighting and offers terms, which they accept, refusing only to fight Croesus; they receive cities that still bear their name. Xenophon assesses the Persian cavalry, scythed chariots and camels.","ideas":["A phalanx of close friends is the strongest","Abradatas dies charging the Egyptian line","Cyrus spares the Egyptians as brave men and makes them friends","Lasting military legacies: cavalry gear and scythed chariots; camels returned to baggage"],"terms":[{"t":"scythed chariots","d":"Chariots fitted with blades that mangled men and arms; Xenophon says later kings kept them."},{"t":"Enyalius","d":"A war god to whom the army raises its battle shout."}]},{"i":1002,"j":1030,"loc":"Book VII 7.2.1–7.2.29","title":"Sardis taken and Croesus learns self-knowledge","summary":"Cyrus marches on Sardis and takes the citadel by a steep path a former slave revealed. He expels the Chaldaeans for leaving their posts to plunder and makes them hand their loot to those who kept guard. Croesus, brought before him, advises against sacking the city: the Lydians will hand over their valuables freely, and the crafts, the source of wealth, will survive. Asked about Delphi, Croesus tells how he tested Apollo, how his sons brought him grief, and how he misread the response to know himself, mistaking his own measure when he made war on Cyrus. Cyrus restores his wife, daughters, friends and table but forbids him war; Croesus compares his new lot to his wife's carefree happiness.","ideas":["Obedience rewarded and insubordination punished over the plunder","Sparing a city preserves the crafts that produce wealth","Croesus's failure to know himself as the root of his ruin","Croesus kept in Cyrus's company thereafter"],"terms":[{"t":"know thyself","d":"The Delphic response to Croesus, which he admits he did not understand until his defeat."}]},{"i":1031,"j":1062,"loc":"Book VII 7.3.1–7.4.16","title":"Panthea's death; Caria, Phrygia, and the march","summary":"While the treasures are packed, Cyrus learns that Abradatas has fallen and finds Panthea by the Pactolus with the body; the dead man's severed hand comes away in his grasp. Panthea blames herself for urging her husband to prove his worth; Cyrus promises honours and an escort, but once he leaves she stabs herself on the body, and her three eunuchs follow her. Adusius then settles the Carian civil war by entering both factions' strongholds on the same night and binding them to peace; the Carians ask for him as satrap. Hystaspas subdues Hellespontine Phrygia, the coastal Greeks agree to pay tribute, and Cyrus, with Croesus and the Sardian wagons, conquers Greater Phrygia, Cappadocia and Arabia on the way to Babylon.","ideas":["Panthea's grief and suicide over Abradatas","Adusius keeps his oath to both Carian parties by reconciling them","Willing subjects keep their arms; resisters are reduced to slingers","Treasure is entrusted to those who deserve to own it"],"terms":[{"t":"satrap","d":"A governor of a province of the Persian empire."}]},{"i":1063,"j":1098,"loc":"Book VII 7.5.1–7.5.36","title":"The capture of Babylon","summary":"Cyrus surveys Babylon's walls and withdraws by folding his thin lines back into a doubled phalanx. Judging the city impregnable to assault, he digs trenches round it and builds towers, while the Babylonians, provisioned for twenty years, mock him. On a night of festival he diverts the river into the trenches, and his troops march up the dry channel; Gobryas and Gadatas lead the way to the palace, where the king is killed. Proclamations keep the people indoors; the citadels surrender at dawn, and the Babylonians give up their arms. The magi choose sanctuaries and first fruits for the gods, houses go to those who earned them, and the Babylonians are set to till and pay tribute.","ideas":["Folding the phalanx to double its depth during withdrawal","Turning the river so the army enters by its bed","The festival night leaves the city drunk and unprepared","Gobryas and Gadatas avenged on the wicked king"],"terms":[{"t":"magi","d":"Persian priests who direct the sacrifices and select the gods' share of the spoil."}]},{"i":1099,"j":1132,"loc":"Book VII 7.5.37–7.5.70","title":"Cyrus withdraws from the crowd and chooses guards","summary":"Cyrus first holds open audience from dawn, but the crowds leave no time for his friends. He asks for counsel: Artabazus humorously recounts how hard it has always been to reach him, and Chrysantas argues that Cyrus, no longer needing to court the multitude, should have a home of his own. Cyrus moves into the palace, sacrifices to Hestia and Zeus, and, judging Babylon hostile, picks eunuchs for his personal service, reasoning that men without wives or children will be most loyal to their patron and are not weaklings. He adds ten thousand Persian spearmen as palace guard and a garrison paid by the Babylonians.","ideas":["A ruler's accessibility suited war but not settled rule","The hearth as the most sacred and dearest place","Eunuchs trusted as guards because they have no rival attachments","Keeping the conquered without resources to keep them submissive"],"terms":[{"t":"Hestia","d":"Goddess of the hearth, to whom Cyrus sacrifices first on entering the palace."}]},{"i":1133,"j":1157,"loc":"Book VII 7.5.71 – Book VIII 8.1.8","title":"To hold an empire requires continued virtue","summary":"Cyrus gathers the peers and tells them their conquests are rightly theirs by the law of war, but that winning an empire is less than keeping it, which needs self-control, temperance and care. Virtue decays when not practised; the ruler must be better than his subjects, share hardships with them, and keep the arts of war for himself. Toil makes good things pleasant, and losing goods hurts more than never having them. The peers are to attend at court as in Persia and educate their sons there. Book VIII opens with Chrysantas comparing a good ruler to a good father and urging willing obedience; the nobles resolve to attend court, a custom Xenophon says persists.","ideas":["Keeping power is harder than winning it","Rulers must excel subjects in virtue and keep the monopoly of arms","Toil gives relish to good things","Free men obey willingly, slaves unwillingly"],"terms":[{"t":"homotimoi (peers)","d":"The Persian nobles educated together who form Cyrus's ruling class."},{"t":"sōphrosynē (self-control, temperance)","d":"The virtue Cyrus names as indispensable for holding what has been won."}]},{"i":1158,"j":1197,"loc":"Book VIII 8.1.9–8.1.48","title":"Organizing the court by system and example","summary":"Cyrus appoints officials for revenue and estates and models his administration on the chain of command in the army, so he deals with few heads and gains leisure. He compels nobles to attend court by seizing absentees' property and favouring those present. He makes himself a model, calling the good ruler a law with eyes: devout to the gods, respectful, obedient-honouring, self-controlled; the court becomes decorous. He trains his men through hunting, adopts Median dress and cosmetics to cast a spell of majesty, keeps servants unarmed but well fed, and resolves to make the powerful nobles friendlier to himself than to one another.","ideas":["Administration copied from the army's hierarchy of ten, hundred, thousand","The ruler as a seeing law and living example","Hunting as the best training for war","Distinguishing considerateness, which avoids the seen, from self-control, which avoids the unseen"],"terms":[{"t":"aidōs (respect, modesty)","d":"The regard for others Cyrus displays so that others will show it to him and to one another."}]},{"i":1198,"j":1225,"loc":"Book VIII 8.2.1–8.2.28","title":"Winning friends by gifts and kindness","summary":"Cyrus wins affection first through care and sympathy, then through gifts, especially dishes from his table, which Xenophon explains are superior because large establishments allow division of labour. His lavish giving and rewards create the king's many eyes and ears, so that no one speaks against him. He likens a good king to a good shepherd. When Croesus warns that giving will impoverish him, Cyrus has his friends pledge sums, which far exceed what hoarding would have produced, and argues that wealthy friends are better treasure and guards than stored gold. He stocks physicians and medicines for the sick, and his contests and choice of judges make the nobles rivals of one another.","ideas":["Specialization of labour in large cities and kitchens","The king's eyes and ears as a network built by rewards","Friends as Cyrus's treasury: the subscription test","Happiest is he who acquires honestly and spends nobly"],"terms":[{"t":"king's eyes and ears","d":"The many informers whom rewards encourage, not a single appointed official."}]},{"i":1226,"j":1275,"loc":"Book VIII 8.3.1–8.3.50","title":"The royal procession; Pheraulas and the Sacian","summary":"Cyrus distributes Median robes and has Pheraulas, a commoner, arrange his first state procession to the sanctuaries. Bulls, horses for the Sun, sacred chariots and fire precede Cyrus in purple and upright tiara; the people prostrate themselves, and petitioners are referred to officers. He dismisses the slow Daiphernes and honours a prompt rider. After sacrifices, horse races follow; a Sacian soldier wins and, throwing a clod blindfold at Cyrus's friends, hits Pheraulas, who keeps riding and receives the horse. At dinner Pheraulas, once a poor farmer, argues that wealth brings more care than joy and hands all his property to the Sacian to manage, keeping only freedom to serve his friends.","ideas":["Ceremonial splendour as an instrument of government","First prostration of Persians before Cyrus","Losing wealth pains more than having it pleases","Pheraulas's view of man as the most grateful creature"],"terms":[{"t":"proskynēsis (prostration)","d":"The act of homage performed before Cyrus for the first time at the procession."}]},{"i":1276,"j":1311,"loc":"Book VIII 8.4.1–8.4.36","title":"The victory banquet and Cyrus's open treasury","summary":"At a victory banquet Gadatas, chief mace-bearer, seats guests by Cyrus's esteem, a precedence that can rise or fall with conduct. Gobryas wonders that Cyrus shares every delicacy and judges him greater in kindness than generalship. Cyrus explains to Hystaspas why Chrysantas ranks above him: he anticipates needs and rejoices in Cyrus's good fortune. Gobryas says he would now more gladly give his daughter, since the Persians bear prosperity with self-control; amid jokes about matching wives, Hystaspas is betrothed to her. Cyrus dismisses his allies with gifts, divides the Sardis spoil by merit through the ranks, and shows his friends his whole wealth, declaring it as much theirs as his.","ideas":["Seats of honour as public, revisable rewards","Bearing good fortune well is harder than bearing misfortune","Chrysantas's initiative earns precedence over mere obedience","Making one's means known so friends can draw on them"],"terms":[]},{"i":1312,"j":1339,"loc":"Book VIII 8.5.1–8.5.28","title":"Ordered camp, Cyaxares' daughter, Persian covenant","summary":"Cyrus sets out for Persia, and Xenophon describes the fixed plan of the royal camp: the king's tent facing east at the centre, guards and cavalry around, hoplites forming a wall, everyone knowing his place so packing and pitching take no longer than for one tent. On the way Cyrus visits Cyaxares, who offers his daughter and all Media as dowry. In Persia Cambyses proposes a covenant: Cyrus will defend Persia and its constitution, and the Persians will uphold his rule; the throne passes to Cyrus at Cambyses' death. Returning through Media, Cyrus marries Cyaxares' daughter and takes her to Babylon.","ideas":["Orderliness as essential in war even more than in a household","Tactics include dividing forces and speed, not only drill","The covenant between the king and the Persians","Media joined to Cyrus by marriage"],"terms":[{"t":"taxis (order)","d":"The fixed arrangement of the camp that lets everyone find his place without confusion."}]},{"i":1340,"j":1362,"loc":"Book VIII 8.6.1–8.6.23","title":"Satraps, garrisons, and the postal system","summary":"Back in Babylon, Cyrus sends satraps to the subject nations but keeps the garrison commanders answerable only to himself, explaining this in advance so no one feels distrusted. He names the satraps, exempts willing allies like Cilicia and Cyprus from Persian governors, and orders all satraps to imitate his court, cavalry, hunting, table and seating of honour. Xenophon notes the annual inspecting army and the relay post stations, the fastest overland travel. Cyrus then conquers lands from Syria to the Indian Ocean and Egypt, rules an empire bounded by heat, cold, water and drought, and rotates between Babylon, Susa and Ecbatana while gifts pour in.","ideas":["Separating civil satraps from military garrisons as a check","Satrapal courts copying the king's","Post stations spaced at a horse's day's ride","The empire's four natural boundaries"],"terms":[{"t":"satrapy","d":"A province under a satrap who governs, collects tribute and pays the troops."}]},{"i":1363,"j":1390,"loc":"Book VIII 8.7.1–8.7.28","title":"The death of Cyrus","summary":"Very old, on his seventh visit to Persia, Cyrus dreams he will soon go to the gods; he sacrifices, thanking them for guidance and for never letting him think beyond human bounds. Failing for three days, he calls his sons and friends, calls himself fortunate, gives the throne to Cambyses and Media, Armenia and Cadusia to Tanaoxares, and urges the brothers to love each other, since faithful friends are a king's truest sceptre. He argues the soul likely survives the body and is most intelligent when freed, citing sleep, but bids them fear the gods in any case. He asks to be buried directly in the earth, says farewell, covers himself and dies.","ideas":["Succession settled by seniority to prevent dispute","Faithfulness is created by kindness, not compulsion","Arguments for the soul's survival: avengers, honours to the dead, sleep","Burial in the earth, which nourishes all good things"],"terms":[{"t":"psychē (soul)","d":"That which gives the body life and, Cyrus suggests, may be most intelligent once freed."}]},{"i":1391,"j":1417,"loc":"Book VIII 8.8.1–8.8.27","title":"Epilogue: the decline of the Persians","summary":"Xenophon states that Cyrus ruled the greatest empire like a father, but that after his death his sons quarrelled, nations revolted and everything declined. He argues that Persians now break oaths, as with the Greek generals of Cyrus the Younger, reward treachery, and extort from the innocent. Old customs survive only in form: they still eat once a day but all day, avoid bringing in drinking pots but are carried out drunk, and no longer hunt; justice at court goes to bribes and the boys learn poisons. They keep Median softness without Persian rigour, make knights of servants, avoid close combat, misuse scythed chariots, and depend on Greek mercenaries. He concludes they are now less pious, dutiful, just and brave.","ideas":["Rulers' character shapes their subjects'","Customs kept in letter but not in purpose","Military decay and reliance on Greek mercenaries","The book's closing thesis of Persian degeneration"],"terms":[{"t":"tryphē (luxury, effeminacy)","d":"The Median softness Xenophon says the later Persians kept while dropping Persian discipline."}]}]});